; Test: COUNT with guard (expected value check)
; =============================================
;
; Demonstrates: Using a constant instead of $k to guard the count
; Based on test from kernel/src/main.rs:1598

; Setup: Initial facts
(foo 1) (foo 2) (foo 3)
(bar x) (bar y)
(baz P) (baz Q) (baz R)

; Test 1: Guard that matches (18 combinations expected)
(exec 0
  (, (foo $x) (bar $y) (baz $z))
  (O (count (all-eighteen) 18 ($z $y $x))))

; Test 2: Guard that doesn't match (16 combinations, but we have 18)
(exec 0
  (, (foo $x) (bar $y) (baz $z))
  (O (count (all-sixteen) 16 (cux $z $y $x))))

; Expected output:
; (all-eighteen)
;
; Explanation:
; - Test 1: count is 18, guard is 18 → MATCH → produces (all-eighteen)
; - Test 2: count is 18, guard is 16 → NO MATCH → produces nothing
;
; The guard (constant in $k position) filters outputs by count
